## Key architectural decisions (settled)

These were settled in conversation and are load-bearing for the work below.
Don't re-litigate; build on them.

- **The brain MCP key is fixed at `brain`.** The entry in `.mcp.json` is
  always keyed `brain`, regardless of which backend (Obsidian today, Notion
  tomorrow) is wired underneath it. This collapses three coupling problems
  at once: (1) agent frontmatter becomes a universal `mcpServers: [brain]`
  with no per-backend rewrite at install time; (2) tool names emitted in
  `aide_brain`'s `instructions` prose are always `mcp__brain__*`; (3)
  detection becomes "look for `brain` in `.mcp.json`" — a single fixed
  lookup. Backend identity is no longer carried by the MCP key — it is
  inferred from the **command/args shape** of the entry under the `brain`
  key. Adding a new backend = teaching the registry a new
  command/args matcher.
- **The brain owns its own navigation rules after the entry-point.**
  `provisionBrain` writes a `CLAUDE.md` to the brain root that tells agents
  which tools to use for further reads/searches/writes. That file IS the
  abstraction layer. The backend driver only needs to know **how to reach
  the entry-point** (which read tool name, which file at root) — once the
  agent reads the entry-point, the brain takes over. Don't over-engineer
  the registry to encode every operation per backend.
- **`instructions` is pre-formatted prose, not structured fields.** The
  server assembles the instruction string per backend; the agent just
  executes it. Server owns backend knowledge, agents stay simple. Avoid a
  structured-fields contract — that pushes backend assembly into every
  consumer.
- **Backend detection: one fixed MCP key (`brain`), backend identified by
  command/args shape.** The registry maps a command/args matcher to its
  driver, not an MCP key to its driver. `buildBrainState` reads the `brain`
  entry from `.mcp.json`, hands the entry to the registry, gets back the
  resolved driver (or `null` if no matcher fires). No `config.aide` needed
  until multiple `brain`-shaped backends are wired simultaneously.
- **`aide_info` stays a fast boot probe.** Brain runtime instructions live
  in `aide_brain`, invoked only when an agent reaches for the brain.
  Boot-time `brain.status` precondition gate stays in `aide_info`.
- **Wired-but-unrecognized collapses to `no-mcp-entry`.** When `mcpServers["brain"]`
  exists but `resolveBackend` returns `null`, the detector returns
  `status: "no-mcp-entry", backend: null` — same vocabulary as
  `aide_info.brain.status`, no new term. Same remediation as "never wired"
  (re-run `/aide:brain config`).
- **Migration path: rename `obsidian` → `brain` at install time.** `provisionBrain`
  emits a `would-overwrite` step for legacy installs during their next
  `/aide:upgrade` or `/aide:init`. No runtime fallback in `buildBrainState` —
  the install service is the one-place migration surface.

